WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.512 --> 00:00:03.220
XIAO-JING WANG: So
it's really a pleasure

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.830
to be here to attend
the inauguration

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.330
of this new Integrative
Computational Science Center.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.660
Of course, MIT is a special
place in this field.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:21.040
I thought I would mention that
Norbert Wiener pioneered what's

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.520
called Cybernetics here at MIT.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.960
And this year is also
the 40th anniversary

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.640
of the publication
by David Marr,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.260
the book called Vision, which
is still very much influencing

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.230
neuroscience and
cognitive science.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.350
If you're familiar
with David Marr's work,

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.210
you probably mostly
remember, especially

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.360
younger people,
that he was really

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.560
a pioneer in computational
cognitive science,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.080
mostly from computational
perspective.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.330
But maybe it's good
to mention that he

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.430
has deep roots in neurobiology.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.100
When he was an
graduate student, he

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.230
published this series of three
papers, really seminal papers,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.150
modeled theories of
hippocampus, cerebellum,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:16.900
and neocortex, based on
anatomy, based on circuits.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.550
Unfortunately, he passed
away at the young age of 35.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:27.140
As Francis Crick, who knew him,
stated, had he lived longer,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.720
he would have moved to
a synthesis of those two

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:32.530
approaches.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.140
So perhaps, today,
we can together

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.950
start to think about how
we can help to realize

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.910
that vision of a synthesis.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:46.360
So of course, today,
we're living in an era

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.620
where AI pervades all
aspects of society.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:57.070
Just to mention that
although today's systems are

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:00.880
tremendously successful in
solving certain problems better

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.180
than humans, you
could say that if it's

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.510
purely fit for the
architecture, then

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.900
when there's no external
input, the system

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.620
is essentially silent.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.450
It doesn't do anything.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.500
So in that sense, you could
say that this type of systems

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.080
don't really have a mental life.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.500
By contrast, thinking,
reasoning, planning--

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.510
somehow, this went away.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:36.430
This microphone stopped working.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.353
Oh, maybe Michael,
he did something.

00:02:40.353 --> 00:02:42.020
AUDIENCE: We need
the last [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:44.662 --> 00:02:46.370
XIAO-JING WANG: But
this stopped working.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.912
AUDIENCE: Yeah, they
turned that off.

00:02:47.912 --> 00:02:48.710
Yeah?

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.120
XIAO-JING WANG: Oh, you
want me to use this now?

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:50.745
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:02:50.745 --> 00:02:51.980
XIAO-JING WANG: OK.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:52.490
Oh, great.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:53.690
OK, sure.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:54.200
That's good.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.000
That solves the problem.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.080
Either way.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.424
Good.

00:02:58.424 --> 00:02:59.650
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.790
AUDIENCE: Can you turn
them down just a little?

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:09.840
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.965
XIAO-JING WANG: No problem.

00:03:10.965 --> 00:03:12.220
No, this is great.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.390
Yeah.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.778
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.947
AUDIENCE: This is [INAUDIBLE]
is through the technology.

00:03:23.947 --> 00:03:26.432
XIAO-JING WANG: [LAUGHS]

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.320
Well, I just hope that Robert
will give me five more minutes.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.250
So I was saying that reasoning,
thinking, planning, feeling

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:42.080
something from your fond memory,
essentially, internal processes

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.360
that are really not
enslaved to the environment.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.770
And so those processes depend
on some brain structures,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.710
especially what's called
the prefrontal cortex.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.890
So maybe a challenge
for us is to think

00:03:54.890 --> 00:04:00.110
about building a theory of
deep, recurrent neural networks

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.250
that are capable of this more
kind of internally driven

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.420
processes important
for mental life.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.990
So if you try to think
into the future what kind

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:17.959
of measure of success for such
a model we could think about,

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:20.750
I will just mention one thing,
repeating what I just said.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.640
Basically, a machine with
human-like intelligence

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.900
should, in principle, without
any external stimulation,

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.700
have something like
reasoning, using logic,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:38.690
maybe creating something new,
and perhaps, even emotional

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.370
feelings out of memory.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.240
So I'm going to tell
you a bit about how

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.750
we start to think about
this type of internally

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.400
driven processes.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.970
First, a little bit of summary
about the micro circuits

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.090
that we have been
thinking about, and then,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.580
spend more time, as
Robert mentioned,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.490
our recent work building
multi-regional, large-scale

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:02.250
models.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.330
So we've got to pay attention
to connectomics, anatomy,

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:09.350
and neurophysiology, and the
biophysical-based models end

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.630
up being known in
dynamical systems.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.660
Let me just give
you, for those of you

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:18.660
who are not familiar with what
we do, a very simple example.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.260
Imagine that you
are asked to make

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.170
a perceptual
decision-making where

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.270
you are shown a display
of random thoughts,

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:31.460
and your judgment is whether
the net motion of the stimulus

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.940
is, A, upward, or, B, downward.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.640
So you can think about
a little microcircuit

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.300
where you have neuron
groups, neuron assemblies,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.960
A or B. Within each, there is
strong recurrent excitation

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.450
between the cells, and
then, the two neural groups

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.200
compete against each other.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.550
So what's interesting about
this type of model, I think,

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.150
is this duality.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.138
So on one hand--

00:05:55.138 --> 00:05:56.180
I don't know if you can--

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.470
actually, do I have a pointer?

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.320
Maybe I just use the cursor.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.230
As you see on the
right, on one hand,

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:06.080
you see this very slow
transient in the form of quasi

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.200
[INAUDIBLE] ramping activity
up to a second, which

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.450
has been shown in
single neurophysiology

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.320
to underline accumulation
of information, accumulation

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.920
of evidence in favor or against
the different options, either

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:22.070
A or B. And, on the
other hand, there's

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.260
this also categorical
choice by winner

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.490
take all competition done
by attractive dynamics

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.840
in this model.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.710
And the same mechanism
can sustain activity

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.240
when the stimulus
is gone, so you

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:39.740
can hold in working memory
your choice for later use.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.010
And also, as I mentioned,
I'm very happy to hear,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.320
is that one big challenge
to understand the brain

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.330
is cross-level understanding.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:53.430
So this kind of model try
to move in that direction.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.780
So in this case, starting
with recurrent dynamics,

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:59.030
you can ask, what's
the mechanism

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.310
underlying slow transients?

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:05.000
It turns out, in this case,
it's NMDAR-dependent recurrent

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:05.820
excitation.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.660
So this is something you
can test using some drugs,

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:14.720
agonist-antagonist.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.820
On the other hand,
you can go up,

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.040
look at psychometric
function to be

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.290
compared with
behavioral measurements

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:22.500
in the experiments.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.950
So this line of research
has led to the idea

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:29.460
that, although the
scheme looks universal,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.870
it could be V1
model, but somehow,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.140
under certain conditions,
the same kind of circuit

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:39.530
can underlying cognitive
kind of computations,

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.400
like working memory
and decision-making.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.770
So over the last, I don't
know, almost two decades,

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:49.490
we have been exploring
different kind of building

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.680
blocks of cognition.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.670
I would say Robert
Young's work in his PhD

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:59.970
really kind of represents
where we are now.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.650
So we ask the question about
how a local circuit, in PFC,

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:08.660
for example, can
underline so many tasks.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.960
So that way, you can
start to ask questions

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.770
like how abstract task
representation really

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.780
is realized by neuron circuits.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.740
One of the open questions
in this line of work

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:27.970
is, what is the dynamical
nature of internal states

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.250
underlying working memory
and decision-making?

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.840
I just want to make a point that
when we say attractor states,

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:37.340
we don't mean necessarily
steady states.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.490
So those are two
examples where you

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:45.310
can have stochastic
oscillations in a network that

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:47.620
are attractor states.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.320
Maybe, for physicists,
even turbulence

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.810
is actually described
as attractor state

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.890
by Navier-Stokes equation.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:58.180
So those are very complex
spatial temporal patterns,

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.050
but they are attractor states.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.920
But what is, exactly,
the nature of

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.110
spatial-temporal neural
activity patterns

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:10.270
that are important for internal
mental processes is not known.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:14.200
So we tried to start
to think about that.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:17.200
But the temporal
aspect of the sense

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.400
is really a very interesting,
still under exploited

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.540
direction.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.690
But for the rest of
the talk, I would kind

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:30.730
move on to the spatial aspect
of cognitive functions,

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:34.180
because we now know, we
become more and more aware

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.480
that even walking
memory of simple stimuli

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.320
involves multiple brain regions.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:42.190
So this is a meta
analysis figure

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:45.490
showing, along the axis,
different cortical areas

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.400
in macaque monkey,
how likely you

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:55.270
would see delay activity in
red in individual experimental

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:57.170
studies.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.940
So it's not uniform
across all cortical areas.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:04.390
Certain areas seem to be
engaged in working memory more

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:06.680
permanently than the others.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:09.580
But on the other hand,
it's also distributed.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.700
And also, Aramina,
for example, showed

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.400
that if you simultaneously
record from multiple brain

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.600
regions, in this case,
six or so cortical areas

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:22.150
in macaque monkey in a
simple decision task,

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.450
you also see very
distributed processes.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.450
So it's a puzzle.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:32.110
How can we understand
distributed working

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.070
memory and decision-making?

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:38.620
So I would say it's timely to
raise this question because

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:46.030
of the advances of experimental
tools and the measurements.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:50.140
So I'm going to tell
you a story from my lab

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:56.290
where we collaborated with Henry
Kennedy, anatomist in France,

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:58.640
who relatively
recently published

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:01.030
a database of
interarea connectivity

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:02.390
in the macaque cortex.

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.960
So basically, I
skipped some details,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.060
but just to tell you
that they had a way

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:10.960
to assess directed and
weighted connectivity

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.370
between cortical areas.

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.780
If you plot the statistics of
the weights in the upper right

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:20.950
panel, you would see five
orders of magnitude range

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:25.570
of the weight values that fit
with log normal distribution.

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:28.600
And also, in the
lower right panel

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.990
is shown the weight of the
connection between two areas

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.960
as a function of the distance
between the two, similar log

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.280
plot, meaning that the network
is really spatially embedding.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.210
It depends on spatial proximity
between the areas, not

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.500
a pure topological
graph, as you would

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.300
describe with a graph theory.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.140
So imagine that you take
this connectivity matrix

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:59.710
to build a dynamical model of
many interacting macaque cortex

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:00.610
areas.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.080
By the way, I also
should mention

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.870
that there was a lot of
feedback projections.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:10.180
About 65 of all possible
connections are present.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.770
So it's kind of a
tense connection

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:14.590
at this level of
cortical-cortical connectivity.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:15.830
So we did that.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.100
So we started with this
connectivity matrix,

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:23.620
and the next question is, what
do you put in each local area

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:25.070
to build a dynamical model?

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.490
So I'm going to give
you three versions how

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.140
we went about doing that.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:32.980
So the first version is to
start with this idea, very

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:36.160
influential idea, that there's
a canonical circuit that kind of

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.530
repeats across the
cortical mantle,

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.670
even across species in
the mammalian kingdom.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.580
So that's saying, basically,
that all cortical areas

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.150
are the same.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.320
What's different is
input-output patterns,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.550
how close you are to census and
how they talk to each other.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:58.450
So that definitely is true,
but it's not the only answer,

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:01.870
I think, because we do
also have to think about,

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:06.010
on the other hand, there's this
idea of modular organization.

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:11.380
[INAUDIBLE],, for
example, proposed that

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:15.160
and showed that for certain
type of information processing

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.600
for sure, right?

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.650
And also, maybe, in
the brain, there's

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:23.050
also some kind of modularity.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:24.850
And so we try to
think about how do you

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.760
square right between on
one hand a universal kind

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:31.760
of canonical circuit
architecture.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:35.960
On the other hand, there's
this modularity of functions.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:38.620
So we are going to take
the view that, yes,

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:40.330
input-output patterns
are important,

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:44.440
but also, what's
been less emphasized

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:46.660
is that, in fact,
quantitatively,

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:50.320
there are variations from
area to area, heterogeneous,

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:51.800
is if you like.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:55.330
So one example would
be, do genomic analysis.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.660
You will see a lot
of gene expressions

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:02.090
that are different
from area to area.

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:06.260
So we actually started by
looking at the order database,

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.290
which is just the spine count.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:11.530
As you know, spines are the
size of excitatory synapses

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:13.030
in pyramidal cells.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:16.665
And there's only one synapse
per spine, so that's very nice.

00:14:16.665 --> 00:14:18.790
You can think about the
number of spines per neuron

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.790
as a proxy for the strength
of excitation of a neuron.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:24.820
And that turns out to change
quite a bit from area to area,

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:29.120
as shown in the lower left panel
as a function of hierarchy.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.350
So actually, the
change is about 10-fold

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.770
from V1, all the way
to PFC. And so this

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:39.940
is one example of what we
call macroscopic gradient.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:43.090
So some biological properties
that change from area to area

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:45.340
are not high-dimensional
heterogeneities,

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:49.270
but they somehow show these
gradients in low-dimensional--

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.330
along the low-dimensional axis.

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:55.750
So if you're interested,
you can take a look

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:59.140
and find out more about
especially the ones that

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:02.680
are important for excitation
and inhibition, which we know

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.280
are functionally important.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:09.430
By this way of
thinking about it,

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:11.860
we actually found a way
to use genomic data, which

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:14.350
is still rare in
computational neuroscience.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.600
So we're kind of
excited by that.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:21.730
In a way, a big challenge is how
to relate from gene to behavior

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.050
through circuits.

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:25.130
So we start to think about--

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:26.740
and that's especially
important if you

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:30.400
care about psychiatric disorders
in computational psychiatry.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.460
Just to mention
that, in my mind,

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:36.580
this is not only a way
to do it, but also,

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.170
a way to do it for a
large-scale system.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:42.250
So, Rishi, who was a
graduate student back then,

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:44.155
built that dynamical
model, basically, I

00:15:44.155 --> 00:15:46.600
explained to you using
the structure data,

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:50.200
and I introduced this
macroscopic gradient

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:53.140
for a dynamical model,
which simply means

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:55.720
for modelers that
you use exactly

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.210
the same mathematical equation
for each local circuit,

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.370
but then, the
strands of excitation

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.160
is scaled by a number that's
proportional to the spine

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:04.150
count.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.590
That's all.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:10.630
And so this is a simulation
of the resting state where

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.720
each area is driven by noise.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.370
The network is not
doing anything.

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:21.040
And he found right away
that the time constant that

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.040
dominates the dynamics is
very different from area

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.860
to area, as quantified
here by autocorrelation

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.980
function of the time
series from each area,

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.580
and as shown on the lower
right, the dominant time

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:37.510
constant for 14 areas as
a function of hierarchy.

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:40.640
Notice that the range
is pretty big, from 20

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.880
milliseconds to more
than one second,

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.580
but it's not a monotonic as a
function of hierarchy, defined

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:46.870
by hierarchy.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:50.800
So this is really a reflection
of both macroscopic gradient

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:55.990
of properties and this
complicated connectivity matrix

00:16:55.990 --> 00:16:57.640
properties.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.400
This kind of hierarchy of
time constants is really,

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.680
I think, very interesting
for a number of reasons.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.890
To start with, you want
early sensitive areas

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:09.640
to be very fast in
order to process quickly

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.770
changing external stimuli.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.780
On the other hand, when
I talk about ramping

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:16.720
activity underlying
accumulation of information

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.020
in decision-making, you have
very slow time constants

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:22.390
in the underlying circuit.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:26.380
So there is now increasing
evidence from single neuron

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:30.520
recordings that there is indeed
a hierarchy of time constants

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.620
in monkey, shown at the top.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:36.850
And those are mice just
a little bit lower left.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.020
And by very different
measure, possibly important,

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:47.110
also, for human, say,
speech understanding.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.060
You started with very
short time constants

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.860
to understand the
words, and then, you

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:55.090
integrate words into phrases,
from phrases to sentences,

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.260
to narratives.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:18:02.830
And there is some evidence that
those different integrations

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:09.450
involve somewhat distinct
speech-related brain regions.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.620
So the second version
of the model we did

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:14.050
is to incorporate
laminar structure.

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:16.660
That's because, as
you know, if you

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:20.400
want to understand
top-down versus bottom-up,

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:23.550
bottom up projection originates
from superficial layer,

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:27.660
and top-down projections
start with deep layers going

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.530
to lower areas.

00:18:29.530 --> 00:18:33.960
And so in order to compare our
model with neurophysiology,

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:39.660
we focused on physiological
studies by [INAUDIBLE] others

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:43.050
showing, basically, that the
feedforward processing seemed

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:47.850
to enhance gamma
oscillation-related coherence,

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.430
whereas top-down
enhances synchrony

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:54.760
in alpha or low beta
frequency range.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.240
So I'm going to skip the
details of this work,

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:02.850
just to show that
that actually led

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:06.480
us to learn a number of things.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:12.210
I want to end this part just
by showing a suggested schema

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:16.290
from this work which could be
relevant to predict the coding.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:19.200
So this is coming
derived from that study.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.610
As you know, predic-coding
idea says that our brain is not

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:25.230
passive, right?

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:29.880
We constantly try
to infer or predict

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.860
what's going to happen
next in the environment.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.370
So that seemed to imply
some kind of comparison

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.130
between expectation
y and the reality

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:41.160
x, something like x minus y.

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:45.630
There may be several ways of
doing that, but what we found,

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:50.140
a possible substrate for
this is shown at the bottom.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.130
So basically, if the
top-down y signal,

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:57.670
the expectation signal
goes to the deep layer,

00:19:57.670 --> 00:19:59.850
which projects to
the inhibitor neurons

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.490
in the superficial layer,
then change the sign

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:07.860
from y to minus y so that
can be compared with x.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:12.150
We are starting to explore
this type of circuits

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:15.060
for predictive coding.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.010
Now, so how about
working memory?

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:22.170
So this is my third kind of
version of the model right now.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:27.420
So now, each local circuit
is a full-fledged nonlinear

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:30.090
multi-attractor network.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:32.070
Actually, the same
model I described to you

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:33.870
briefly at the beginning.

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:36.120
Remember, I showed an
example with random dots.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:39.800
It's exactly the same local
cycle model now incorporated

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.940
into each local circuit
in a large-scale system.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.150
So again, the scheme
looks universal, so

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.590
canonical circuit idea.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:49.110
And again, you have a
gradient of the strengths

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:51.930
of excitation and inhibition.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:53.760
And now, we want
to apply this model

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:56.310
to distributed working memory.

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:58.570
And two papers just came out.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:01.300
So let me just show
you one example.

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:04.320
In a model, what's useful
about a competition model,

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:06.510
I guess, is to look
at different regimes.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:10.470
So this is one example when
the local recurrent connection,

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:13.710
even in PFC, is
not strong enough

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:15.580
to sustain [INAUDIBLE] activity.

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:18.000
So whatever you see
should be the result

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:22.080
of long distance connection
loops between cortical areas.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:28.110
So this is a simulation of a
visual delayed response task

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.030
where you put the input of V1.

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:33.300
You should see that V1
MT show a strong response

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:36.690
during stimulus, but decays
away right away right.

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:42.510
Whereas LIP and frontal areas
show this sustained activity

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:44.730
when the input is gone.

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:47.430
In the lower right, you
see this activity map.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:48.960
During the stimulus,
the activity

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:52.200
is mostly in the posterior
parts of the cortex,

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:56.280
but during the delay, you see
distributed working memory

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.850
that engages prefrontal
cortex, parietal cortex,

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:02.730
and some infratemporal areas.

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:06.450
If you're now just
using the same data,

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:09.930
plot the fire rate as a
function of the hierarchy

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:13.440
for all cortical areas,
then it's pretty clear

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.740
that those areas that are
engaged in working memory, that

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.710
are involved in working
memory representation

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:21.690
are separated from
those that are not

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:24.720
by a gap in the fire rate.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:27.570
So if you're familiar
with dynamical systems

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:30.120
theory in the local
circle models,

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.090
remind you of
bifurcation diagram.

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:36.210
Except, now, if it
is a bifurcation,

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:39.030
it's something that is
new that I didn't really

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:40.740
find in the
mathematical literature

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.920
because we're talking about
a bifurcation in space.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.410
X-axis is the hierarchy.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.820
You have strongly
recurring networks.

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:52.770
Different areas are talking
to each other through loops.

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:58.440
It's not independent systems
where you change a parameter.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:22:59.750
And that's not the case.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.500
So you have this strong
recurring network.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:03.810
So one prediction
about the bifurcation

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:06.570
of phase transition in
physics is this idea

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:09.900
that you will have
a divergence of time

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:12.760
constants, a so-called
critical slowing down.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.900
And indeed, we find
that in abstract model.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:19.290
and that's very interesting,
because as I showed you

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.080
in the resting state,
the time constant that

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.560
dominates dynamics should be
more or less monotonic going up

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:26.910
along the hierarchy.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:31.200
But during the delay
period, the model predicts,

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.690
the bifurcating
space idea predicts

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.930
that you actually have this
type of pattern for the time

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:37.650
constant.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.930
When you're near the
transition, the time constant,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:43.800
in principle, could go to
infinity, mathematically

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:44.430
speaking.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.920
And that can be tested in
single neuron recordings.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:52.380
So we also found a number
of self-sustained attractor

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:53.160
states.

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:58.200
Some of them cannot be triggered
by stimulus to a sensory area,

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:01.680
so we don't quite understand
the functional meanings of those

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:02.550
attractor states.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.910
But if you remember what I said
at the beginning, I think this,

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.550
really, potentially,
is a start to think

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:11.910
about all kinds of
internally driven processes

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:16.950
involving different
subsets of areas.

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:19.890
To my surprise,
certainly, the model

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:26.640
can account basically the
results of the meta analysis

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:31.320
from more than 90 monkey
physiological studies,

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.870
validating this kind
of approach, at least.

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:35.860
Now, let me end
with one more thing,

00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:40.210
which is, how do you study
distributed decision-making?

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:42.070
So we look at
signal propagation,

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:44.250
which is an old problem
in computational science.

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:47.850
But now, you revisit it,
spike in your own version

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:52.080
of the model, and you give
a very brief input to the UI

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.140
and look at how
spikes propagate,

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:56.820
as shown on the left.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:01.740
Now, what's unexpected
is the plot on the right,

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:03.750
especially if you look
at the peak response

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.840
as a function of the
strength of input.

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:09.310
For prefrontal
cortex, in red, there

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.910
seem to be some kind
of threshold in effect.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:13.800
So if you are below
the threshold,

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:17.580
basically, you don't gain access
to PFC. There's nothing in PFC.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:24.210
But if you are above it, you
will see that PFC lights up.

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:27.070
The system seems to
become a lot more active.

00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:30.690
That's reminiscent of what's
called the ignition phenomena,

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:35.310
if you heard about it, proposed
by Stanis Dehaene and others,

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:40.330
the so-called global neuronal
workspace idea for awareness.

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.610
So the idea is tha--

00:25:42.610 --> 00:25:45.880
actually, maybe I'll
show you over here.

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:50.640
So imagine that you just
do a detection task.

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:54.810
This is a monkey experiment
using a detection task

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:57.990
where you just change the
contrast of visual stimulus,

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:01.260
and below is the
psychometric function.

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:05.430
So if you adjust the
contrast, right in the middle,

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:07.560
near the threshold,
detection threshold,

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:10.890
now with the same physical
stimulus, sometimes you see it,

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:12.840
sometimes you don't see it.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.240
In fact, there are
four types of trials.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:18.720
You have hit, miss, but
also, without any stimulus,

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:21.270
you might have
correct rejection,

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:23.070
or you can have false alarm.

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:26.760
What they found in
this study, recording

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:30.150
from four different areas,
is that in V1 and V4,

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:36.180
neurons seem to reflect the
physical presence of stimulus

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:40.290
in hit and miss trials, but
in PFC, the activity pattern

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:45.090
is very different depending
on subjective awareness

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:46.710
of the stimulus.

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:50.670
And so the same model that I
talked to you about actually

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:59.130
can reproduce, essentially, the
trend across cortical areas.

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:02.940
We're not saying that we have
a model for consciousness,

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.900
but we have model for
distributed decision-making

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:07.170
now, perhaps.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:10.500
Started with detection,
which perhaps is the simplest

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:12.540
decision process,
but we can start

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:17.950
to think about more and
more complex decision tasks.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:24.510
Let me maybe end by
one speculative slide

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:27.120
about abstraction.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:30.150
This is jumping
ahead, but maybe it's

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:33.240
appropriate for this occasion.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:36.720
How we can really think
about really simple things,

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:41.790
gradually towards more complex,
more abstract abilities that

00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:43.680
are uniquely human?

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:47.940
So perhaps we can think about
two types of abstraction.

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:51.330
One involves more
temporal trajectories,

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:55.740
like how do you organize
cooking a meal, for example,

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.210
occur in a sequence.

00:27:57.210 --> 00:27:59.790
And the other one
is more in space.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:02.700
I'm thinking about a work
like Tim Behrens and others,

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:04.050
how we can--

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:06.240
Stefano Fuizzi, right?

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:09.870
How do you think about
abstraction and geometric

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:12.870
organization of abstraction,
and maybe a combination

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:16.200
of those two kinds of
abstractions really

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.070
underline increasingly
more complex

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:21.900
and interesting
cognitive abilities.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:25.700
So let me end by saying,
again, congratulations.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:31.746
And I'm sure the center will
become an icon of the field.

00:28:31.746 --> 00:28:34.722
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:41.488 --> 00:28:42.280
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:45.840
I think we will start with
some audience questions.

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:48.360
We have a 15 minute
Q&amp;A, and then

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.810
I also have some prepared big
picture questions if we have

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:53.310
time.

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:03.708
There is a--

00:29:03.708 --> 00:29:06.397
XIAO-JING WANG: Yeah.

00:29:06.397 --> 00:29:07.230
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:29:10.195 --> 00:29:13.810
AUDIENCE: You talk about
auto-correlation times

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:15.910
in these different
regions, and I

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:19.390
think that's getting at
sort of a related question

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:23.650
to your sort of starting
point where you've posited

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:27.940
that this sort of
internal life of the mind

00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:31.990
may be generated in
these higher level areas.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:37.420
Do you have data on spontaneous
patterns of activity

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:42.460
that are present outside
of stimuli in cortices,

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:46.990
like the prefrontal
cortex, that may

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:50.615
drive this sort of cogitation?

00:29:50.615 --> 00:29:51.490
XIAO-JING WANG: Yeah.

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:56.230
I did show a slide of tests
of this hierarchy of time

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:59.710
constants using
single neuron data.

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:02.630
So people have looked at that.

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:06.700
But I think you have
more asking if you

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:11.560
put an electrode in, let's
say, humans' speech areas,

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:15.670
do you see this internal
integration process?

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:18.970
I'm sure there will
be those kind of data,

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:20.530
but I don't think--

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:22.105
I think it's a start point.

00:30:22.105 --> 00:30:23.980
We're just starting to
get this kind of data.

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:29.290
There's also, just
to mention, that even

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:31.870
in simple working memory task--

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:35.320
I'm thinking about nice
work by John Murray,

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:39.760
articulating that,
during the delay,

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:41.860
there's some manipulation
of information.

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:45.010
Maybe you are doing some
logical computations,

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:48.100
converting from here
to there, and that's

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:49.930
internal computation.

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.790
And you could look
at time constants

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:55.950
in different parts of the
brain during this process.

00:30:55.950 --> 00:30:56.450
Ann?

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.580
Ann, yeah?

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:01.350
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
Very interesting talk.

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:05.240
I wonder how--
you've talked about

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:09.450
cortical-cortical
connectivity and spatial math

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:12.870
for [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:17.530
I started with a compliment.

00:31:17.530 --> 00:31:18.740
It's a beautiful talk.

00:31:18.740 --> 00:31:24.770
And then, you have spoken of
cortical-cortical connectivity

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:29.210
mainly and the kinds of maps
that one can find, either

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:30.950
spatial or temporal.

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:33.740
I just wonder, there are
so many recurrent loops

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:36.830
from down below the
neocortex proper.

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:41.810
I'm sorry to ask, but
they give every appearance

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:46.280
of being extremely powerful and
having some of the properties

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:50.460
that you've ascribed to
the neocortex itself,

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:52.575
including distributedness
and modularity.

00:31:52.575 --> 00:31:53.450
XIAO-JING WANG: Yeah.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:59.490
So I mentioned-- thank
you, Anne, for that point.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:02.840
Definitely, of course,
subcortical structures

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:04.650
are important.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:07.430
I mentioned that we started
using this new database

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:09.350
from Kennedy's group.

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:11.150
Of course, ideally
we hope that we

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:14.510
could use connectivity
data obtained

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:16.950
using the same
type of measurement

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:21.590
so it will be uniform,
high-quality data.

00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:25.010
And to my knowledge, those data
are not quite available yet,

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:25.820
quantified.

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:29.780
I'm sure you did
a lot of anatomy.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:32.160
Susan Haber, for example,
had a lot of data,

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:34.080
but not quite in
the same format,

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:35.810
same kind of quantification.

00:32:35.810 --> 00:32:37.410
We'd love to do that.

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:42.860
That said, you have, indeed,
the same kind of data for mice.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:45.290
So we started to
actually also build

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:49.050
a model for multi-regional
model of the mice brain,

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.230
including subcortical
structures.

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:53.930
Actually, we also
started to look

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:55.910
at plasticity in the models.

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:57.620
It's very exciting, I think.

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:03.590
AUDIENCE: Xiao-Jing,
yeah, great talk.

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:07.850
So as you know, there's been a
lot of work showing sequences

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:10.160
in different parts of
the brain and cortex.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:13.010
Gilles Laurent just recently
had a really beautiful paper

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:16.280
showing long sequential
activations in intact turtle

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:17.600
brain.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.400
Do you think
there's a connection

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:22.280
between the timescale
on which sequences

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:25.550
could persist in these
different hierarchical areas?

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:27.590
I mean, you focused on
persistent activity,

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:31.100
but sequences are a different
form of persistent activity.

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:34.790
Do you have any
thoughts on that?

00:33:34.790 --> 00:33:36.140
XIAO-JING WANG: Not a lot.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:39.080
So that definitely is a very
interesting open question

00:33:39.080 --> 00:33:43.340
to us, especially when you think
about multi-regional systems.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:47.090
Maybe just to mention that
Yuri Hanson I showed you

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:50.750
one plot by his work
on human speech,

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.960
he used the term
"time integration"

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:58.250
as if it's like a sequence
in speech, it's a sequence.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:03.890
But there's no real mechanistic
kind of models, at least,

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:04.980
in this context.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:06.830
And of course,
there are good works

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:12.889
on local circuits in songbirds
and in parietal cortex,

00:34:12.889 --> 00:34:14.600
[INAUDIBLE].

00:34:14.600 --> 00:34:17.900
So we start to think about
this, but we don't really

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:19.442
have clear ideas.

00:34:19.442 --> 00:34:20.900
I mean, one
interesting question is

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:24.409
that you have this canonical
circuit organization,

00:34:24.409 --> 00:34:26.690
and then, the question
is, how do you endow

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:29.570
and how do you explain
different areas that

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:35.150
may operate in different
dynamical regimes or modes?

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:40.100
Sequence versus oscillations,
versus some other type

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:41.495
of dynamical regimes.

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:46.790
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:49.933
I was hoping some
general questions, but--

00:34:49.933 --> 00:34:51.350
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
mean, maybe this

00:34:51.350 --> 00:34:53.653
is partly a general
question, so I'll ask it.

00:34:53.653 --> 00:34:56.070
Xiao-Jing, I have, I guess,
two general questions for you.

00:34:56.070 --> 00:34:58.610
So one is, I guess, related
to Michael's question.

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:01.910
So there is this hierarchy
that you describe beautifully,

00:35:01.910 --> 00:35:06.750
and it was really interesting
modeling work on that front.

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:09.253
And so I guess Michael's
question is about,

00:35:09.253 --> 00:35:10.670
as you go up the
hierarchy, do you

00:35:10.670 --> 00:35:12.260
see longer and longer sequences?

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:14.240
And an alternative
question related to that

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:17.840
is, is that the way that we
should think about hierarchy,

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:20.402
or should we think about
hierarchy as in maybe we

00:35:20.402 --> 00:35:21.860
should expect to
see similar length

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:23.722
sequences or similar
types of activation

00:35:23.722 --> 00:35:25.430
in the different areas,
but maybe they're

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:27.600
representing bigger and
bigger chunks of the world?

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:30.460
And so to the point
of your plots,

00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:34.910
you did see an increase in
time scale as you go up,

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:37.250
and you showed us your
computational plots

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:41.720
where you get this divergence
of time scales, but divergences,

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:46.110
I mean, it's very fine-tuned
to get very long time skills,

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:50.070
but at the same time I can
think about things on seconds,

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:53.130
minutes, days, years, decades.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:54.430
And so where are those--

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:56.180
that's something that's
always puzzled me.

00:35:56.180 --> 00:35:57.860
Where are those hierarchies?

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.685
Like, where do we get
six orders of magnitude?

00:36:00.685 --> 00:36:02.810
Do you think that's coming
from the same processes,

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:04.727
or do you think that
when we think cognitively

00:36:04.727 --> 00:36:08.625
about six orders of magnitude
in time, it's something else?

00:36:08.625 --> 00:36:09.500
XIAO-JING WANG: Yeah.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:13.490
So I'd love to talk more,
offline, maybe, more, but let

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:17.120
me just quickly try to
answer your question.

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:19.370
Actually, now I think
about it, maybe just

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:25.550
to offer my current take,
which may a mistake,

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:27.530
is that if you really
think about sequence,

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.780
I want to think about
two kinds of sequences.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:34.430
One is really like a play cell
kind of sequence in real time.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:37.190
The other one kind
of transitions

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:39.590
between quasi-stable states.

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:43.280
That's different kind
of, I think, sequence,

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:47.870
perhaps more relevant
to behavior or thoughts.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:48.560
Sorry.

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:51.320
Behavior, by that, I
mean maybe movements,

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:56.060
sequence of discrete
movement primitives.

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.520
So I don't know if you
think about the first time

00:36:58.520 --> 00:36:59.480
or the second time.

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:01.100
So about the time
constants, it's

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:03.410
more relevant for
the first time.

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:06.330
so I mentioned the example
of predictive coding.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:08.060
So for example, this
would be a really--

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:09.860
I think it's a good example.

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:12.920
So if you try to predict
when you watch a movie,

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:16.100
you need to know the temporal
context, what happened before.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:18.530
So that's why you
need this long time

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:22.880
constant in high areas that has
this information about the past

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:28.100
to tell the downstream
system what to expect.

00:37:28.100 --> 00:37:31.872
So that's the, I
guess, one time.

00:37:31.872 --> 00:37:33.830
AUDIENCE: And I guess
the question I was asking

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:35.480
is mechanism.

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:37.640
Do you think we understand
mechanism for getting--

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:38.030
XIAO-JING WANG: Sorry.

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:39.560
AUDIENCE: --very
long timescales?

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:41.630
XIAO-JING WANG: So I
want to really say this.

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:43.280
Cortical [INAUDIBLE]
down does not

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:44.630
need any tuning of parameters.

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:46.755
That's especially interesting
about the [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:46.755 --> 00:37:49.850
in space because, if you like--
it's like your parameter is

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:52.370
in space.

00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:54.530
So if you change
any parameters, you

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:58.283
may move that location a
bit, but it's always there.

00:37:58.283 --> 00:38:00.200
AUDIENCE: You always get
the long time scales.

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:01.880
Very cool.

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:02.660
AUDIENCE: Right.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:04.340
I'll just take the
remaining two minutes

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:06.890
and ask a general question.

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:09.140
It's kind of
unusual, but can you

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:12.950
tell us something, maybe a
story about the human side

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.470
behind the science?

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:18.650
Like, we have lots of
junior people here.

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:22.640
Is there some difficulty that
you and people in your group

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:24.680
had to overcome to do
some of these sciences?

00:38:27.225 --> 00:38:28.100
XIAO-JING WANG: Whoa.

00:38:31.893 --> 00:38:34.310
AUDIENCE: Or maybe I can give
you a prompt, given that I--

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:38.570
so the first--

00:38:38.570 --> 00:38:40.250
XIAO-JING WANG:
Actually, I don't mind.

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:41.810
This might be-- yeah, go ahead.

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:44.060
AUDIENCE: So the first
part and the second part

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:46.080
is pretty different, right?

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:49.610
So when you're starting the
research of the second part,

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:51.634
what are some challenges there?

00:38:51.634 --> 00:38:56.870
Is there any challenge
finding, like, essentially

00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:58.310
starting a new direction?

00:38:58.310 --> 00:38:59.300
XIAO-JING WANG: Yeah,
maybe I actually

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:00.590
will start with the first one.

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:02.460
I mean, I don't
mind to tell you.

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:04.965
Is that what you are asking,
a bit more personal question?

00:39:04.965 --> 00:39:05.590
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:06.090
Yeah.

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:07.430
Yeah.

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:11.540
XIAO-JING WANG: So how
did I get into PFC, right?

00:39:11.540 --> 00:39:14.990
The long shot of it is that
I switched from physics

00:39:14.990 --> 00:39:19.370
to neuroscience, as Robert
mentioned, in the lab of Walter

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:21.530
Freeman at UC Berkeley.

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:25.580
It turns out that his father was
the infamous Walter Freeman who

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:29.370
undertook frontal lobotomies
for thousands of people,

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:32.840
including the sister of
JFK, Rosemary Kennedy.

00:39:32.840 --> 00:39:38.050
So I got to learn about the PFC
in his lab, kind of talking,

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:39.650
telling you about stories.

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:41.910
And then, probably,
I lost my job.

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:46.400
I couldn't find a place to go.

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:50.430
And I went [INAUDIBLE]
to a summer school.

00:39:50.430 --> 00:39:53.550
That was the first time
that, of course, took place.

00:39:53.550 --> 00:39:55.850
And that's where
I met John Renzo.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:57.590
I was telling him I
don't have any place

00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:02.900
to go after the summer school,
and he facilitated, helped me

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:05.420
to find a place in his group.

00:40:05.420 --> 00:40:10.510
And I actually started to meet
with Bob, if you remember,

00:40:10.510 --> 00:40:14.090
at NH, and I started to
talk to psychiatrists.

00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:19.160
That's how I got into
the mystery of PFC,

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:21.000
very accidentally.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:21.860
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:40:21.860 --> 00:40:22.790
Thank you, Xiao-Jing.

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:23.570
Thank you.

